    {
      "pmcid": "PMC12071709",
      "figure_number": 11,
      "source_figure_id": "healthcare-13-01071-f011",
      "source_caption": "The abdominal CECT of a 72-year-old man who presented in the emergency room with acute abdomen and SBO revealed an irregular stenotic thickness at the level of a distal jejunal loop, as seen in image ( A ). Additionally, image ( B ) displays a coronal plane where irregular tissue in the nearby mesenteric fat (red arrow) is clearly visible, with a high grade of obstruction (red star) as a sign of persistent blockage. This high grade of obstruction is also evident on the axial plane in image ( C ). The imaging findings suggest neoplastic stenosis from adenocarcinoma, with a differential diagnosis of a carcinoid tumor. Histology confirmed intestinal adenocarcinoma with infiltration of the mesenteric tissue.",
